topics.description:
A class is syntax over prototypes. This reference lists all members you can write.

```js
class Child extends Parent {
  publicField = 1;              // instance field
  #secret = 2;                  // private field
  static count = 0;             // static field
  static #registry = new Map(); // private static
  static { Child.ready = true; }// static initialization block

  constructor(x) { super(x); }
  method() {}
  #privateMethod() {}
  static create() { return new this(); }
  get value() { return this.#secret; }
  set value(v) { this.#secret = v; }
  [Symbol.iterator]() { /* computed name */ }
  async load() {}
  *gen() {}
  static async *stream() {}
}
```

### Rules
| Topic | Rule |
|---|---|
| Hoisting | class declarations are in the TDZ before their line |
| Strictness | class bodies are always strict mode |
| Calling | a class cannot be called without `new` |
| `extends` | accepts any expression returning a constructor or `null` |
| `super()` | required in a derived constructor **before** touching `this` |
| `super.method()` | calls the parent version, also works in object methods |
| `#x in obj` | brand check for private fields |
| `static` | members live on the class itself, inherited by subclasses |
| Class expressions | `const A = class Named {}` |
| Fields | initialised in order, after `super()` returns in derived classes |
| Methods | non-enumerable, live on the prototype |
| Fields | own, enumerable properties of each instance |

Arrow function fields (`handler = () => {}`) bind `this` per instance, at the cost of one function per object.

